bill@west.msi.com (Bill Poitras) writes:

>Surya N Kavuri (kavuri@lips2.ecn.purdue.edu) wrote:
>:   
>:   When I leave a level with some corpses of dead creatures
>:   and return after a long time, the display does not clear
>:   the corpses until I reach the spot.
>
>This is a normal behavior of object display.  When ever you discover the
>location of an object, it stays on the map until you discover otherwise.
>In 3.0, whenever an item was moved, or picked up, it would disappear,
>which would give you information that you really shouldn't have known
>about.  I think that corpses rot even when you are not on a particular
>level.  Its like they have a time stamp (like a death certificate I
>suppose) so when you come back to the level the current time is
>subtracted from the current time and if its too long the corpse
>disappears.  This is from observation of the game not the source.
>
>Nethack 3.1 has cured a lot of inappropriate or incosistant behavior that
>many people (including myself in some cases) may not realize right away.


I wish that this applied to monsters travelling staircases, too.  On
every occasion that I've followed one on the stairs, the monster is
on the next level immediately adjacent to the staircase, no matter
how long I've waited to follow after him.  If it was running from me,
shouldn't it have run as far from the staircase as possible?  This
could be accomplished by applying a timestamp to the monster when
it used the stairs, and looking at the current time when I follow
it to its level, computing how far it should have travelled based
on the time difference.

-jim

